WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:16.132
BG: My name is Brooke Gardner and I'm a graduate student at UCSF.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:19.999
and I'm here at the Marine Mammal Center in the Marin Headlands.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.066
talking to Kajusa Brejc who is a staff scientists at Berkeley and HHMI.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:30.466
First, can you tell us a little bit about yourself and your work at Berkeley?

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.432
KB: My background is structural biology and biochemistry

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:38.666
and currently I work in the laboratory of Professor Barbara Meyer

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:45.166
whose lab is interested in dosage compensation and sex determination of C. elegans.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:49.432
BG: And how did you become involved in volunteering at the Mammal Center?

00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:54.066
KB: When I moved to the Bay Area from Europe back in 2001,

00:00:54.067 --> 00:01:01.632
I was thinking of volunteering at a non-profit organization that involved work with the animals.

00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:08.199
So, I was looking around and cam upon a notice on the Marine Mammal Center

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.099
I got interested and attended their orientation and decided to join.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:19.199
So, starting from September 2002, I have been volunteering at the Center.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.266
BG: Can you tell us what you do as a volunteer?

00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:27.099
KB: I am part of the group of volunteers on the Wednesday night crew

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:33.432
at the Marine Mammal Center and we're involved in the husbandry part.

00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:38.899
So that means we are taking care of the animals while they're here at the Center.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.366
BG: Can you tell us about what the mission of the Center is?

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:46.899
KB: The mission of the Center is the rescuing and the rehabilitation of the marine mammals.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:53.266
The Center is also doing the scientific research as well as doing the education of

00:01:53.267 --> 00:02:00.766
the public about the marine mammals, their health and the health of their environment, the ocean.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.499
BG: Can you tell us what types of animal are represented here?

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:13.199
KB: Marine Mammal Center rehabilitates California sea lions, harbor seals, fur seals,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.999
sea otters, dolphins, and there are also cases of whales.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.132
BG: Co you want to describe for us what you do on a shift?

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:27.266
KB: Our shift starts with preparing all the food for the animals

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:33.632
and depending on the time of the season we would either prepare

00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:37.732
the formulas which are made from salmon oil and milk powder

00:02:37.733 --> 00:02:42.332
or the food would be fish, typically herring.

00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:47.032
So depending on what stage the animals are, we feed them appropriately.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.032
Then we would feed the animals at particular time points.

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:53.866
So that means our feeding times are 8 o'clock and at midnight.

00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:58.699
If animals' needs requires some medication, that will also be taken care of.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:06.332
Afterwards, we feed the animals. We observe the animals that everything went fine.

00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:12.566
We have to clean the pans, we have to sanitize the pans and make sure that the pans are clean.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:20.999
We will have to clean all our dishes and utensils, record all the observations,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.999
and make sure that we have followed all the recommendations from the vet staff,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.199
and so basically that...we repeat that at 8 o'clock and at midnight.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.432
And we finish the crew.

00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:36.632
BG: How does the Center identify seals that need rescuing and how do these animals come to you?

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:42.499
KB: If the general public finds an animal that's alone on the beach

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:48.466
or in places where it's not supposed to be, like on the street, close to humans,

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:54.199
then people would call the Marine Mammal Center or notify other agencies

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.399
and they would get in touch with the Marine Mammal Center

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:04.032
who would then send volunteers to collect the animal and bring it to the Center.

00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:10.166
There are also cases where animals are entangled in the nets

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:16.099
or in some kind of marine debris and they would be also rescued from the water.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.166
So there are different ways of how the mammal are found.

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:23.332
BG: Can you tell us how you train these pups to fend for themselves in the wild?

00:04:23.333 --> 00:04:27.299
KB: We are trying to minimize contact with the animals as much as possible

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.266
so that the animals don't get dependent on humans.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:36.032
So that means we would not talk while in the presence of the animal.

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:42.266
We would handle the animal as quickly as possible during the feeding time.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:45.232
We would stay away from the fence

00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:51.066
and we would try to avoid that the animals associate food with humans.

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:55.732
So that means in the case of the California sea lions,

00:04:55.733 --> 00:05:04.132
we try not to, for example, have a fish in a hand and show it to the seal

00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:08.566
because California sea lions are extremely smart animals

00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:10.432
and they can make that connection.

00:05:10.433 --> 00:05:17.032
So, we are trying to keep them as wild as possible during the time they are at the Center.

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.032
BG: How long are they usually at the Center?

00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:23.032
KB: Harbor seals, depending on their condition,

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:25.966
whether they are sick, whether they have an infection

00:05:25.967 --> 00:05:28.966
or whether they are just being separated from their mothers,

00:05:28.967 --> 00:05:31.966
they can stay at the Center...and depending on their progress,

00:05:31.967 --> 00:05:40.332
how well they gain weight and they learn to feed, they can stay at the Center between 2 to 3 months.

00:05:40.333 --> 00:05:41.699
BG: Where do you release them?

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:50.599
KB: The release of the animals happens at the remote beach where there is not much of human traffic

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:55.332
and usually they are released in a group of 2 to 3 animals

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:59.632
depending on how many animals are ready to be released at that given time.

00:05:59.633 --> 00:06:01.099
BG: Do they each get names?

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:08.366
KB: Each of them gets a name. Their named by the rescue volunteer group.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:13.632
And they are very creative names.

00:06:13.633 --> 00:06:17.599
And each of the animals have their own personalities as well.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.266
So it's very easy for some animals to be recognized every week

00:06:22.267 --> 00:06:25.866
when we come back to see them and recognize them.

00:06:25.867 --> 00:06:28.099
BG: How many animals are usually here at one time?

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:32.199
KB: On average years we would have up to 35 animals on site

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.999
but there are a couple of years where we had almost 70 animals.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.332
BG: Do you see any parallels between working in lab and working with the harbor seals?

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:44.066
KB: No, none.

00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:49.199
No, let me rephrase that. With the worms or with bacteria is does not matter, right?

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.499
Yeah, you repeat the experiment...it's bad but it's no big deal, right?

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.266
Here, when you put the tube into the animal,

00:06:58.267 --> 00:07:03.899
you really want to make sure that you're in the right spot and that you are not harming the animal

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:11.799
because it would be devastating to lose an animal due to a mistake, human mistake.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.332
BG: So, when you have a long day in the lab, this is completely different afterwards?

00:07:16.333 --> 00:07:18.332
KB: Oh, yeah!

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:21.399
BG: Do you think it helps to have something that's separate?

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:27.899
KB: Yes. It puts everything into perspective. It gets you out of the lab.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:36.099
You can do something else and you meet new people from different walks of life.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:43.066
And we have been now, most of our crew, almost 8 years together.

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:49.499
So we work well as a team. So you get to learn how to work with other people from different backgrounds.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.732
BG: What's your favorite part of volunteering?

00:07:51.733 --> 00:07:55.466
KB: Depending on the time of the season, there are different things.

00:07:55.467 --> 00:08:02.166
Being outdoors because this is a really beautiful spot where the Marine Mammal Center is located.

00:08:02.167 --> 00:08:08.132
Working with the animals and having seen them progress and eventually be released is

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:10.200
really very rewarding.

